WEBVTT

resistance-04-1
00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:07.040
Previously: those who hold on and those
who strive are both anxious.

resistance-04-2
00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:13.800
This time, a stranger question: can order
itself feel anxious too?

resistance-04-3
00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:19.970
Episode 4: Can Order Feel Anxious Too?

resistance-04-4
00:00:21.270 --> 00:00:28.392
When we say ‘order is anxious,’ it is
easy to imagine a giant subject worrying

resistance-04-5
00:00:28.392 --> 00:00:29.670
like a person.

resistance-04-6
00:00:29.670 --> 00:00:34.200
This series sees it differently. Order is
not a giant. It is a set of arrangements:

resistance-04-7
00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:38.348
who is recognized, who is seen, who is
entitled to speak, and whose concerns

resistance-04-8
00:00:38.348 --> 00:00:39.330
receive a hearing.

resistance-04-9
00:00:39.330 --> 00:00:44.126
These arrangements depend on particular
people to sustain them through

resistance-04-10
00:00:44.126 --> 00:00:44.880
repetition.

resistance-04-11
00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:49.110
The author’s definition of ‘order’

resistance-04-12
00:00:50.210 --> 00:00:55.140
Back to the street dance club. Where is
the club’s ‘order’?

resistance-04-13
00:00:55.140 --> 00:00:57.800
‘That has always been the rule.’

resistance-04-14
00:00:57.800 --> 00:01:02.177
It is not in any one person’s mind. It is
in the roster the president posts, the

resistance-04-15
00:01:02.177 --> 00:01:06.390
boxes the officer ticks, and an older
member’s ‘It has always been this way.’

resistance-04-16
00:01:06.390 --> 00:01:12.140
Each repetition says: this arrangement
still holds.

resistance-04-17
00:01:13.240 --> 00:01:19.210
‘I do not want to dance in the club’s
style, and I do not accept this ranking.’

resistance-04-18
00:01:19.210 --> 00:01:24.909
These words hurt no one and break no law.
Yet the people sustaining the arrangement

resistance-04-19
00:01:24.909 --> 00:01:26.900
all stop what they are doing.

resistance-04-20
00:01:26.900 --> 00:01:31.610
‘What if everyone starts doing this?’

resistance-04-21
00:01:32.710 --> 00:01:37.254
Order does not fear danger—danger has an
object; it can be guarded against and

resistance-04-22
00:01:37.254 --> 00:01:37.720
blocked.

resistance-04-23
00:01:37.720 --> 00:01:43.252
It fears refusal: power rests on mutual
recognition. When people stop recognizing

resistance-04-24
00:01:43.252 --> 00:01:46.940
their assigned identities, some become
‘unmanageable.’

resistance-04-25
00:01:46.940 --> 00:01:49.110
Author’s OSIPP draft, 2B(i): order is
anxious about refusal. When people reject

resistance-04-26
00:01:49.110 --> 00:01:51.389
assigned identities, some subjects become
‘unmanageable’ (the author cites Foucault

resistance-04-27
00:01:51.389 --> 00:01:52.460
1991, pp. 102–103, on governmentality).

resistance-04-28
00:01:52.460 --> 00:01:57.793
So ‘order’s anxiety’ means the anxiety of
those who assign and sustain these

resistance-04-29
00:01:57.793 --> 00:02:00.810
identities when the identities are
refused.

resistance-04-30
00:02:00.810 --> 00:02:04.560
This series’ proposition

resistance-04-31
00:02:05.660 --> 00:02:10.031
After anxiety comes action: those
sustaining the arrangement explain rules,

resistance-04-32
00:02:10.031 --> 00:02:13.760
screen who can go onstage, and meet to
discuss possible changes.

resistance-04-33
00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:18.657
Notice: they are responding not to ‘the
new member as a person,’ but to a

resistance-04-34
00:02:18.657 --> 00:02:21.340
question: can this arrangement continue?

resistance-04-35
00:02:21.340 --> 00:02:28.896
Pause for a moment: is there a rule
around you that everyone follows, though

resistance-04-36
00:02:28.896 --> 00:02:31.580
no one can say who made it?

resistance-04-37
00:02:32.680 --> 00:02:37.996
Calling order ‘anxious’ does not treat it
as a person with a mind. It refers to the

resistance-04-38
00:02:37.996 --> 00:02:40.430
people and institutions sustaining it.

resistance-04-39
00:02:40.430 --> 00:02:48.110
Nor does every refusal shake the order.
Most are turned away with ‘It has always

resistance-04-40
00:02:48.110 --> 00:02:49.550
been this way.’

resistance-04-41
00:02:50.650 --> 00:02:53.490
This episode’s comparison card (take a
screenshot)

resistance-04-42
00:02:53.490 --> 00:02:56.878
Order is an arrangement — Rosters,
attendance sheets, eligibility to speak.

resistance-04-43
00:02:56.878 --> 00:02:57.420
Not a giant.

resistance-04-44
00:02:57.420 --> 00:03:00.390
Sustained through people’s repetition —
Posting rosters, ticking boxes, ‘It has

resistance-04-45
00:03:00.390 --> 00:03:02.720
always been this way.’ Each repetition
says, ‘It still holds.’

resistance-04-46
00:03:02.720 --> 00:03:06.119
Order fears refusal — ‘I do not accept
this ranking.’ Danger has an object;

resistance-04-47
00:03:06.119 --> 00:03:06.890
refusal does not.

resistance-04-48
00:03:06.890 --> 00:03:12.032
Order’s anxiety — Those sustaining it all
stop what they are doing. It is the

resistance-04-49
00:03:12.032 --> 00:03:14.570
anxiety of those assigning identities.

resistance-04-50
00:03:15.870 --> 00:03:18.260
Order has no heart.

resistance-04-51
00:03:18.260 --> 00:03:24.030
But it has many hands. When refusal
comes, these hands stop together.

resistance-04-52
00:03:24.030 --> 00:03:27.830
The chair is still there.

resistance-04-53
00:03:28.930 --> 00:03:30.490
Next time:

resistance-04-54
00:03:30.490 --> 00:03:36.560
Assigned Identity: Why Are Some People
Dissatisfied?

resistance-04-55
00:03:38.860 --> 00:03:43.770
This is UjiBricks.
